  "name": "PORPHOBILINOGEN (PBG) QUALITATIVE URINE",
  "code": "1182",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>1. Positive test results should be confirmed by quantitative PBG &amp; Porphyrin estimation.<br>2. Negative results should be confirmed by quantitative PBG if suspicion of Acute Porphyria is high.<br><strong>Comments</strong></p>\n<p>Porphobilinogen (PBG) is a porphyrin precursor excreted in urine in patients with Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP),<br>Porphyria variegate (PV) and Hereditary coproporphyria. Acute attacks of Porphyria in individuals with the trait can be&nbsp;caused by drugs like Barbiturates, Sulphonamides, Aminophylline, Griseofulvin and Oral contraceptive agents which&nbsp;increase the synthesis of 5-ALA and PBG. In general, PBG is the more important of the precursors whereas ALA&nbsp;measurement is used as a supplemental test.</p>",
